# Shell scripts must land on disk with LF, on every OS.
#
# Found while proving the grading guard survives a fresh clone: `git clone` on a
# Windows box with the (very common) global `core.autocrlf=true` checked out
# scripts/render_grade.sh with CRLF, and bash then read the `\r` as part of the
# command --
#     scripts/render_grade.sh: line 15: set: -\n: invalid option
#     scripts/render_grade.sh: line 16: cd: $'scripts/..\r': No such file
# -- so the script died on its second line. Every .sh in this repo (the render
# chains, the proof harnesses, the gate wrappers) had the same fate waiting.
#
# Zero churn to apply: every tracked .sh is already LF in the index (checked with
# `git ls-files --eol -- '*.sh'` -- 0 CRLF entries). This only pins the checkout
# so autocrlf cannot undo it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Patches must land on disk with LF too, for a near-identical reason found the
# near-identical way (2026-08-09): a handoff patch generated on this box came out
# CRLF and `git apply` rejected every hunk against an LF worktree --
#     error: while searching for: ... DistanceFog,?  );?
# -- the `?` being the CR git prints when the context line does not match. A patch
# checked out with autocrlf=true would fail the same way for whoever applies it,
# which is a broken handoff that looks like a stale patch.
*.patch text eol=lf

# .bat is the mirror image of the .sh rule above, and it bit exactly as hard
# (2026-08-10). PLAY.bat was first written LF-only; cmd.exe does not read a
# batch file line-at-a-time, it seeks byte offsets, so LF-only lines get split
# mid-token --
#     'm' is not recognized as an internal or external command
# -- that is `rem` torn into `re` + `m`, and `@echo off` never took effect, so
# the launcher printed its own comment block as errors. It only worked here
# because core.autocrlf=true happened to restore CRLF on checkout; on a box with
# autocrlf=false the same commit ships a launcher that cannot run.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
